5	/**
6	 * Living Works — name later, and withdraw (Phase 2, Slice 2).
7	 *
8	 * PATCH  — the member names a work they left unnamed, or renames one.
9	 * DELETE — the member withdraws the declaration.
10	 *
11	 * NAMING LATER is the whole point of the optional title. A work exists when it
12	 * is declared; it is named when the member knows what it is. Those are
13	 * different moments (ledger D-16), which means the second one needs its own
14	 * route rather than being folded into creation.
15	 *
16	 * WITHDRAWAL — exact semantics, because the word could mean several things:
17	 *
18	 *   It removes the WORK, and with it everything that hangs from the Work row.
19	 *   It does not delete the member's writing, and it is not an archive.
20	 *
21	 *   - Manuscripts, drafts, revisions, sources and keeps are untouched by
22	 *     construction: nothing here writes to them, and `member_manuscripts` has
23	 *     no foreign key to a Living Work. A manuscript does not know it belonged
24	 *     to anything, so it cannot lose anything when the work goes.
25	 *   - EVERYTHING that references the Work row with ON DELETE CASCADE goes with
26	 *     it: the declarations of membership (`living_work_expressions`), and since
27	 *     September 2026 also the conversations that belong to the Work row itself
28	 *     (`ask_threads.living_work_id`; a conversation about a WRITING belongs to the
29	 *     manuscript, carries no Work id, and stays), what the
30	 *     writer told MAIA about it (`living_work_writer_understanding`), the
31	 *     writer's corrections, the Work's materials and their considerations, its
32	 *     editorial relationship record and where the writer last returned, and its
33	 *     image (the row cascades; the BYTES are queued below and destroyed by the
34	 *     sweep). The authoritative list is lib/writersStudio/__tests__/
35	 *     removalCopyDrift.test.ts, which fails when a table is added without the
36	 *     member-facing copy being revisited.
37	 *   - It cannot be undone. The member may declare a new Work, but the name, the
38	 *     conversations and the corrections of this one are not kept. This route
39	 *     does NOT soft-delete, because a `withdrawn_at` column would be a status,
40	 *     and status is excluded from this ontology.
41	 *   - The image bytes are the one part that is not transactional. If they are
42	 *     not yet destroyed when the response is written, the response says so
43	 *     (500 {removed:true, refusal:'image_bytes_owed'}) instead of reporting a
44	 *     plain success: the row is gone, the bytes are queued, and the member is
45	 *     not told it is finished until it is.
46	 *   - The removal is RECORDED, in the same transaction: the recovery-capture
47	 *     advisory lock first, then the Work row, and once the row is really gone a
48	 *     `member_deletion` manifest and a `living_works` tombstone
49	 *     (lib/manuscript/source/workDeletionRecord.ts). Without the tombstone a
50	 *     governed restore from a backup taken before the removal would bring the
51	 *     Work back. The independent R20 ledger is NOT sealed here (see that module).
52	 */
